# =============================================================================
# DATA VERSIONING — Git LFS
# =============================================================================
# Datasets and generated artefacts are stored via Git LFS rather than as plain
# Git objects. Git stores a small pointer file; the binary content lives in LFS.
#
# WHY: Git is built for line-by-line text diffs. A CSV that changes every run
# bloats history permanently, because every version is kept forever. LFS keeps
# the repository small while still versioning the data.
#
# Setup (one time):
#   git lfs install
#   git add .gitattributes && git commit -m "Track data files with Git LFS"
#
# Git LFS is used rather than DVC: DVC needs a separate remote (S3/GDrive) to be
# useful, while LFS works directly with GitHub and needs no extra service. For a
# project of this size that is the proportionate choice.
# =============================================================================

# raw and processed datasets
*.csv        filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.parquet    filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.xlsx       filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# generated figures
*.png        filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# keep JSON reports as normal Git objects: they are text, they diff usefully,
# and reviewing what changed between runs is genuinely valuable.